{
  "plan": {
    "overview": "Starting a small indoor herb garden is a great way to bring some greenery into your home and have fresh herbs on hand for cooking. With a few simple steps, you can create a thriving indoor herb garden that will provide you with a constant supply of fresh herbs.",
    "rationale": "Indoor herb gardens are perfect for small spaces and can be customized to fit your specific needs and preferences. They also provide a great way to grow your own herbs year-round, regardless of the season or weather outside."
  },
  "steps": [
    {
      "title": "Choose the Right Herbs",
      "detail": "Select a variety of herbs that you use frequently in your cooking, such as basil, mint, cilantro, parsley, and rosemary. Consider the amount of light and care each herb requires, and choose herbs that are suitable for indoor growing."
    },
    {
      "title": "Prepare the Containers",
      "detail": "Choose small pots or containers that are at least 6-8 inches deep and have drainage holes in the bottom. Fill the containers with a good quality potting mix, and make sure the soil is moist but not waterlogged."
    },
    {
      "title": "Plant the Herbs",
      "detail": "Gently remove the herbs from their pots and plant them in the prepared containers. Plant them at the same depth as they were in their original pots, and water them well. Make sure the soil is moist but not waterlogged."
    },
    {
      "title": "Provide the Right Conditions",
      "detail": "Most herbs require bright, indirect light to grow well. Place the containers near a sunny window or under grow lights. Keep the soil consistently moist, but not waterlogged. Herbs also require good air circulation, so make sure there is enough space between the containers."
    },
    {
      "title": "Maintain the Garden",
      "detail": "Check the soil daily and water the herbs when the top inch of soil feels dry. Fertilize the herbs regularly with a balanced fertilizer. Prune the herbs regularly to encourage bushy growth and prevent them from becoming leggy."
    }
